+
+**Authority-update invariant and profile-specific ordering (R04-01).** Acknowledge effectiveness only after the authority change and corresponding named revisions are durable and the matching authoritative sender view has been published under the applicable first-write fence. First write cannot interleave with a partially applied authority/view update. For the **single-process standalone SQLite profile**, the DEV-07 ordering conforms: commit authority data, audit and named revisions atomically while holding the existing gate, publish the matching sender view/revocation counter before releasing it, then acknowledge. A definitively rolled-back transaction leaves the prior durable state and view in force; a stalled commit blocks sends. Retain the bounded DEV-07 evidence (assurance register E-11); DEV-12 need not replace this mechanism merely to satisfy this architecture. For **PostgreSQL or replicated profiles with network-bound or unbounded persistence**, commit durably outside the local first-write lock, then publish a coherent, non-regressing view through that profile's separately qualified end-to-end fence, then acknowledge effectiveness. Qualify competing publications, the commit-to-publication interval, failure and restart before relying on that sequence; an earlier commit alone is not acknowledged effective revocation. No database I/O runs under the first-write lock in those profiles. Key-service calls and connection, response and drain waits remain outside the first-write lock in every profile.
+
+A definitively failed, rolled-back update receives no success acknowledgement and retains the coherent prior durable state and view. An uncertain commit, or a committed change whose view cannot be published consistently, withholds success and closes affected admission/release until consistency is recovered; it never pretends that a durable revocation was undone. On restart, reload and validate durable authority before enabling any send. A crash after a possible send preserves uncertainty and exposure; absence of a later write receipt cannot justify replay. Qualification follows the profile actually used: DEV-07's bounded queued-revocation and ordinary-restart observations do not establish crash safety or the replicated publication protocol.
+
+**Authority revision vector.** This is a coherent snapshot of the existing applicable named revisions—policy, recovery policy, registry/configuration, source permission, principal/grant/delegation, credential authorization/account scope, price/usage-contract eligibility, and the bound plan-signing key version's verification/revocation eligibility—not an additional master counter. The key-status dependency is not the active minting-key selector: scheduled rotation alone leaves an already-issued unexpired plan eligible; a change that withdraws its verification authority invalidates it. Qualified commission and activation inputs join when enabled. A vector comparison can identify invalidation, but does not replace the fence, expiry, remaining balance, capacity or single-send ownership checks. Preserve implementation-private legacy counters and current strict checks until their explicit successor is tested. Publication, rotation and effective revocation follow their own contracts; rollback cannot resurrect revoked authority.
 
 This local Choice B mechanism is the concrete standalone boundary. A PostgreSQL/replicated authority must separately qualify an equivalent end-to-end release fence, coherent revisions, atomic reservations and ownership recovery. A per-process lock or a shared version store alone is not the distributed proof.
 
